  "description": "A parametric DAYLIGHT PORTAL on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A SEGMENT, in a window opening. It is the sky, at the glass — the area source a renderer wants there, rather than a sun somewhere outside the model.\n\nA SKY HAS NO OUTPUT. It has an ILLUMINANCE, and what arrives through the hole depends on how big the hole is. That is the whole difference between this and every other fitting in the library, and it is why the input is in lux and the result is in lumens: 10 000 lx on 1.44 m² of 0.7 glass is 10 080 lm, and the same sky on a bigger window is more.\n\nTHE PLANE SITS IN THE OPENING, NOT ON THE WALL. Set in by the reveal. A deep reveal cuts the sky the room can see, and putting the source at the glass rather than at the wall line is what makes that cut happen by itself instead of being remembered.\n\nA PORTAL EMITS NOTHING. In Cycles a portal is a hint — \"the light comes from here, sample it\" — and a portal that also emits is the same window counted twice. `portalOnly` therefore drives `lampDimmed` to zero while leaving `portalLumens` reported, so the number is still there to look at.\n\nIT KNOWS WHICH WAY IT FACES, off the host segment. A north light and a west light are the same geometry and completely different rooms, and the wall already knows which it is.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: a 1.2 m opening, sill 0.9 m, head 2.1 m — 1.44 m² glazed, 10 080 lm through at 6500 K under a CIE overcast sky.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. No sun position, no sky gradient, no daylight factor — the first two belong to `render-look` and the third needs the room, which a segment-hosted object cannot see.",
